+*   **Boundary Handling:** At boundaries ($x=0, y=0, z=0$), the neighbor terms are set to 0 (after mean subtraction). This ensures the prediction error is orthogonal to the input variables as per the derivation [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** The algorithm requires four full scans of the volume [S1, unofficial]. The calculation of optimal predictor coefficients takes approximately 6% of the total compression time [S1, unofficial].
--   **Memory Complexity:** Low memory footprint during the main encoding pass as residuals are not stored [S1, unofficial]. Requires storage for the volume data and frequency counts [S1, unofficial].
--   **Performance:** Achieves approximately 50% lossless compression on medical volume datasets [S1, S2, unofficial]. On the `3dhead.vox` dataset (HP 9000 Series 735), compression took 4.7 minutes and decompression 1.4 minutes [S1, unofficial].
--   **Comparison:** Outperforms generic algorithms (gzip, zip, compress, pack) which only remove statistical redundancy, by also removing spatial redundancy [S1, unofficial].
--   **Entropy:** Typical entropies for test datasets range from 3.28 to 10.29 bits/voxel [S1, unofficial].
--   **Limitations:** The assumption of wide-sense stationarity limits optimality on non-stationary data [S1, unofficial]. The fixed 6-neighborhood predictor may be less effective than 18- or 26-neighborhood predictors, though the latter were not implemented in the core algorithm [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Compression Ratio:** Achieves approximately 50% compression (i.e., reduces file size to ~50% of original) on medical volume datasets [S1, unofficial]. For example, on `3dhead.vox`, it achieved 48.5% compression compared to 36.1% for gzip and 33.8% for zip [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On a HP 9000 Series 735, compression of `3dhead.vox` took 4.7 minutes, and decompression took 1.4 minutes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Entropy:** The method reduces entropy to near the theoretical limit. Test datasets showed entropies ranging from 3.28 bits/voxel (`sod.30.vox`) to 10.29 bits/voxel (`hipiph.vox`) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Significantly outperforms generic compressors by removing spatial redundancy. The optimal predictor automatically compensates for non-isotropic voxel sampling [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High computational cost due to multiple volume scans and covariance matrix solving. Assumes stationarity, which may not hold for complex anatomical structures. The truncated Huffman code requires a fixed $L_1$ parameter, which is arbitrarily set to 256 [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
--   **Origin:** Introduced by James Edwin Fowler and Roni Yagel in January 1995 at the Proceedings of the 1994 Symposium on Volume Visualization [S1, unofficial].
--   **Context:** Developed to address the high storage requirements of volume data in biomedical and scientific applications where lossless compression is mandatory [S1, unofficial].
--   **Development:** The method combines Differential Pulse-Code Modulation (DPCM) with Huffman coding [S1, unofficial]. It was compared against generic compressors and simple fixed-coefficient predictors (e.g., $a_1=a_2=a_3=1/3$) [S1, unofficial].
+The method was introduced by James Edwin Fowler and Roni Yagel in January 1995 at the Proceedings of the 1994 Symposium on Volume Visualization [S1, unofficial]. It was designed specifically for lossless compression of biomedical volume data (MRI, CT, electron-density maps) where lossy compression is unacceptable [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm combines Differential Pulse-Code Modulation (DPCM) with Huffman coding, leveraging the spatial coherence of volume data [S1, unofficial].
 
